WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.257 --> 00:00:01.340
SAAD ALI: Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:04.690
We're here at KubeCon
Barcelona with Matt Moore.

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:08.200
Matt is one of the
co-founders of Knative,

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.590
as well as GCR, the
Google Container Registry.

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:14.920
Matt, let's talk about
GCR in the early days.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.702
Where did it start?

00:00:16.702 --> 00:00:18.160
MATT MOORE: So
around the same time

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.910
that Kubernetes was
starting up and Google

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:24.110
was looking at Docker, which
was popularizing containers,

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:27.265
it became pretty clear that
if we launched a container

00:00:27.265 --> 00:00:29.515
service, we were going to
need a way for our customers

00:00:29.515 --> 00:00:32.200
to have really good
integrated experience

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.740
to ship their container
images to that service, right?

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.120
And so a few of us started to
motivate and build that team.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.680
And we were tracking
the project 7.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.280
And then Kubernetes
worked very closely,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.920
because we believed it was
going to be a big deal.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.950
It's kind of hard to imagine it
would become this big a deal.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:56.230
But yeah, like, we were in
on the kool-aid pretty early.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:01.570
And yeah, it's been amazing
to see GCR and Kubernetes,

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:04.940
well beyond that, just
become incredibly successful.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.230
SAAD ALI: Yeah, it is amazing.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.960
And now you're involved
with a new project, Knative.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.890
Can you tell us a little
bit more about that?

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.400
MATT MOORE: Sure.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.730
So GCR sort of led me to
doing a whole bunch of stuff

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:16.630
with container tools.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.360
And I've been working
with [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.720
and our boss, Tomas, for many
years in the container space.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.170
And a couple years ago--

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.330
a year and a half to
two years ago now--

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:33.550
we had this sprint,
where we wanted

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:37.355
to look at what it would
be like to build serverless

00:01:37.355 --> 00:01:39.430
on Kubernetes.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:41.600
And there was some
early prototyping,

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.800
and then a sprint that
ultimately led to us deciding,

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.232
yeah, we're going to do this.

00:01:46.232 --> 00:01:47.440
We're going to do this thing.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.965
And that ultimately
became Knative.

00:01:50.965 --> 00:01:51.590
SAAD ALI: Cool.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.500
So what are some of
the biggest challenges

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.450
that Knative deals with?

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.450
MATT MOORE: So there are
the three main areas--

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.810
build, serving, and venting.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:03.880
I think there are
a lot of things

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:08.020
that were really hard problems
a couple of years ago.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:10.270
I think in the build
space, some of the stuff

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:12.850
that's happened in the
build working group--

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.980
on-cluster builds seem
much more tractable

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.900
now than they did a year ago.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:22.630
And serving, scaling, as rapidly
as a lot of people expect from

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:26.260
serverless-- cold starts,
things like that--

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:27.833
it's still a really
hard problem,

00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:29.500
and it's going to be
one of those things

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.680
that zero millisecond
startups aren't going to be

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.230
fast enough for some people.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:37.030
So it's going to be
one of these things

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:39.160
that we're going to have
to invest in forever, just

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:42.280
like Kubernetes
and scale, right?

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:45.160
And then eventing,
right, like there's going

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.915
to be an endless source of--

00:02:46.915 --> 00:02:48.540
sources, where people
are going to want

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:54.280
to be like, when that happens,
I want to do something.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.370
So there are these
interesting and huge problems.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:59.950
And then even just
organizationally,

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:02.140
how are we going to manage
the complexity of all

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:04.520
of these things over time?

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:07.455
So a lot of
interesting challenges.

00:03:07.455 --> 00:03:08.080
SAAD ALI: Cool.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.710
So what is the current status
of the Knative project, and what

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:14.080
are the features that
you're excited about?

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.730
MATT MOORE: So we just cut
0.6, I think, last Tuesday.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.250
It is my favorite
release so far.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:26.210
We launched the beginnings
of our beta API there.

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:29.140
And we hope that that will
be a near-final API shape

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.730
that we can take through to V1.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:32.980
And a bunch of other
really cool features

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:36.400
launched, like auto-TLS
and some other things that

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.750
allow you to get a really good
experience all the way through

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:44.800
to TLS-terminated
URL, with little

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.840
to no work on the
developer side,

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.290
once an operator has sort
of set things up for you.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:54.040
Looking at that core
of functionality

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.180
that we've been really
iterating on so far,

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.330
we want to get that to GA.

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:00.580
And then we can start
to look at iterating

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:03.880
on improving some of the stuff
around scale, performance,

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:08.770
as well as starting to get into
the rhythm of feature track

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:10.940
releases for new stuff.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:12.220
There's always new stuff.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:14.950
SAAD ALI: Now you have a
managed version by Google

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:16.990
called Cloud Run of Knative.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:19.060
Why a managed solution?

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:24.230
MATT MOORE: So as with
Kubernetes and Istio,

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.550
getting things set up
just right isn't always

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:28.980
the easiest thing in the world.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.980
And I think [? Kelsey ?]
has a great--

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:33.790
Kubernetes the hard way, right?

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:36.730
Thankfully, it's not that
hard, because Kubernetes has

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:38.440
done a lot to get that set up.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:42.160
But I mentioned DNS and TLS.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.220
Getting that set up just right
on the cluster is pretty hard.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:50.650
And we want the managed
service to make that,

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:55.860
the out-of-the-box
experience, really easy,

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.600
so that you can focus on the
things that you actually want

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:03.980
to focus on, and not
the boring stuff.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:06.167
We want to solve the
boring stuff for you.

00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:08.000
SAAD ALI: So let folks
focus on the problems

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.465
that they care about,
rather than infrastructure.

00:05:10.465 --> 00:05:11.090
That's awesome.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:12.110
So last question.

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:13.520
We're here at KubeCon.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.480
This is the biggest
KubeCon so far.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:20.050
What are you excited
about seeing this week?

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.730
MATT MOORE: I think the most
surreal thing has been--

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:24.470
you know, you walk
around, and when

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:27.080
you hear people talking
about this project

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:30.410
that you've helped start and
they don't realize you're

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:33.340
there, that's pretty cool.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:34.880
And seeing product
announcements,

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.560
like the [? Rancher ?]
announcement

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:40.700
I think yesterday
or today, where

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:44.360
they're launching things based
on Knative that you didn't even

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:44.860
know about.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:46.320
And it's great.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.420
It's really cool to
see those things.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.620
But honestly, one of my
favorite things to do here

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:55.070
is to talk to all of the vendors
that are here at the booths,

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:58.520
and see what they're doing,
and find ways to collaborate

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:00.830
and help build the community.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:03.230
That's definitely one of
my favorite things to do.

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:05.810
And connect with
contributors who

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:08.150
I feel like I know
really, really well

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:10.850
but am meeting for the
first time in person.

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:13.970
There's a few of them that I've
just met for the first time

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:14.510
this week.

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:17.710
And yeah, they're great people.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.630
And I love working with them.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:21.380
SAAD ALI: Thank you
so much for your time.

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:24.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

